   "wikipedia": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unreal_World",
   "resumo_en": "Unreal World is the fourth studio album by alternative rock band the Godfathers, released by Epic Records in March 1991. \nIt was produced by Steve Brown (Wham!, the Cult, Manic Street Preachers), who had worked as a mixing engineer on the Godfathers' second album Birth, School, Work, Death. Unreal World marked the first Godfathers album to feature guitarist Chris Burrows, who replaced original member Kris Dollimore, and the last studio album with original members Michael Gibson (guitar) and George Mazur (drums). The album includes a cover of the Creation's 1968 single \"How Does It Feel to Feel\". Vic Maile, who produced the Godfathers' first three studio albums, had worked as an engineer on the original version by the Creation. Maile died of cancer in July 1989, shortly after completing work on the Godfathers' third album More Songs About Love and Hate.\nGraeme Kaye in Q magazine noted that the album \"builds on the same steadfastly foundations as its predecessor.\"",
